fs/fat: terminate the retained chain after shrinking a file

fat_dirshrink() marks the last retained cluster with value zero before
freeing the remainder of the chain.  Zero means that the cluster itself is
free, not that it terminates an allocated chain.  The directory entry keeps
pointing at a chain whose final cluster is therefore reported as unallocated.

This is reproducible with SQLite VACUUM on FAT: shrinking a seven-cluster
database to six clusters succeeds, but the first later write that reaches the
sixth-cluster boundary fails with EIO because fat_extendchain() reads zero
from the cached tail cluster.

Use the same all-ones EOF marker used by fat_extendchain() when it allocates a
new cluster.  fat_putcluster() masks that value appropriately for FAT12,
FAT16, and FAT32, so the retained chain remains valid and the removed suffix
can be reused independently.

--- a/fs/fat/fs_fat32util.c
+++ b/fs/fat/fs_fat32util.c
@@ -1552,11 +1552,11 @@ int fat_dirshrink(struct fat_mountpt_s *fs, FAR uint8_t *direntry,
 
       if (remaining <= clustersize)
         {
-          /* No.. then nullify next cluster -- removing it from the
-           * chain.
+          /* No.. then terminate the retained chain before freeing its
+           * old suffix.
            */
 
-          ret = fat_putcluster(fs, lastcluster, 0);
+          ret = fat_putcluster(fs, lastcluster, 0x0fffffff);
           if (ret < 0)
             {
               return ret;
